## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `SerializerTests.cs`

**Summary: 5 Critical · 0 High · 1 Medium · 0 Low**

This suite is dominated by **self-referential / tautological assertions** — tests that assert `output == input` using inputs that are already in the expected output form, or that round-trip through lossy transformations. These tests give false confidence: they pass regardless of whether the production code is correct.

---

### 🔴 Critical — False Confidence

#### 1. `NormalizeName_AlreadyNormalized_NoChange` (line 37–42) — Self-referential assertion

The input `"alice"` is already lowercase and trimmed. `Normalize()` does `.Trim().ToLowerInvariant()`, so the output equals the input by definition. This test would still pass if `Normalize` were a no-op `return name;`.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — tautological, input is already normalized
var name = "alice";
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize(name);
Assert.AreEqual(name, result);

// ✅ Fix — use an input that requires actual transformation
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize("  ALICE  ");
Assert.AreEqual("alice", result);
```

---

#### 2. `UpperCase_ToUpper_Works` (line 72–77) — Self-referential assertion

The input `"HELLO"` is already uppercase. `.ToUpper()` returns `"HELLO"` unchanged. The assertion `Assert.AreEqual(input, result)` is always true.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — input is already uppercase, so ToUpper is a no-op
var input = "HELLO";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual(input, result);

// ✅ Fix — use lowercase input, assert against a literal expected value
var result = "hello".ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual("HELLO", result);
```

---

#### 3. `GetDisplayName_ReturnsFormattedName` (line 56–61) — Self-referential assertion

`GetDisplayName()` returns `Name` verbatim. The assertion `Assert.AreEqual(user.Name, display)` asserts a property against itself through a trivial passthrough. The test name promises "FormattedName" but no formatting is verified.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — asserts field == field through a passthrough
Assert.AreEqual(user.Name, display);

// ✅ Fix — assert against an independent literal
var user = new UserDto { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 };
var display = user.GetDisplayName();
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", display);
```

---

#### 4. `Validate_ValidInput_ReturnsSameInput` (line 63–69) — Self-referential assertion

`ValidateEmail` returns the input unchanged on success. `Assert.AreEqual(input, result)` is tautological for valid input. Worse, the suite **never tests the rejection path** — no test verifies that invalid emails throw `ArgumentException`.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — only tests that valid input passes through unchanged
Assert.AreEqual(input, result);

// ✅ Fix — assert the literal, AND add a rejection test
Assert.AreEqual("hello@world.com", result);

// Add a separate test for the negative case:
[TestMethod]
public void ValidateEmail_InvalidInput_Throws()
{
    Assert.ThrowsException<ArgumentException>(
        () => Validator.ValidateEmail("not-an-email"));
}
```

---

#### 5. `MapToDto_MapBack_RoundTrip` (line 46–53) — Lossy round-trip / self-referential

The mapping is **lossy by design**: `ToDto` drops `Id` and `Email`, setting `Age = 0`; `ToEntity` drops `Age`, setting `Id = 0` and `Email = ""`. A round-trip assertion on a lossy transform is fundamentally broken — it either silently hides data loss or fails for the wrong reason. The test never verifies the intermediate DTO has correct values.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — round-trips through a lossy mapping
var backToEntity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto);
Assert.AreEqual(entity.Id, backToEntity.Id);       // 1 vs 0 — data lost
Assert.AreEqual(entity.Email, backToEntity.Email);  // "a@b.com" vs "" — data lost

// ✅ Fix — test each direction independently with literal expectations
[TestMethod]
public void MapToDto_MapsFieldsCorrectly()
{
    var entity = new UserEntity { Id = 1, FullName = "Alice Smith", Email = "a@b.com" };
    var dto = Mapper.ToDto(entity);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", dto.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, dto.Age);   // Age has no source — document this
}

[TestMethod]
public void MapToEntity_MapsFieldsCorrectly()
{
    var dto = new UserDto { Name = "Alice Smith", Age = 30 };
    var entity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", entity.FullName);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, entity.Id);     // Id has no source — document this
    Assert.AreEqual("", entity.Email); // Email has no source — document this
}
```

---

### 🟡 Medium — Maintainability

| # | Test | Anti-Pattern | Fix |
|---|------|-------------|-----|
| 6 | `Clone_ProducesEqualObject` (line 28–34) | **Missing reference-inequality assertion** — `Clone()` could return `this` and the test would still pass. | Add `Assert.AreNotSame(original, clone);` to verify a new instance is created. |

